First, consider the inner fraction $\frac{1}{\sqrt{5} + 2}$. To rationalize this, multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:
$$\frac{1}{\sqrt{5} + 2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{5} - 2}{\sqrt{5} - 2} = \frac{\sqrt{5} - 2}{5 - 4} = \sqrt{5} - 2.$$

Substitute this back into the original expression:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1+ \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}+2}} &= \frac{1}{1 + (\sqrt{5} - 2)} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{5} - 1}.
\end{align*}

To rationalize the denominator of the final expression, multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{5} - 1} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{5} + 1}{\sqrt{5} + 1} &= \frac{\sqrt{5} + 1}{5 - 1} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{5} + 1}{4} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{\sqrt{5} + 1}{4}}.
\end{align*}

